# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2
A(('h1', S2))
A(('p', '2.1 The scene. Luke places Jesus in Jericho. “And Jesus entered and passed through Jericho” (19:1). A man named Zacchæus, “the chief among the publicans,” who “was rich,” climbs a sycomore tree to see Him (19:2–4). Jesus says to him, “Zacchæus, make haste, and come down; for to day I must abide at thy house” (19:5). “And when they saw it, they all murmured, saying, That he was gone to be guest with a man that is a sinner” (19:7).'))
A(('p', '2.2 What Jesus says at the house. Zacchæus says, “Behold, Lord, the half of my goods I give to the poor; and if I have taken any thing from any man by false accusation, I restore him fourfold” (19:8). Jesus says, “This day is salvation come to this house, forsomuch as he also is a son of Abraham” (19:9), and “For the Son of man is come to seek and to save that which was lost” (19:10). These verses are reported here as the scene in which the parable is told, and they are kept in their own place.'))
A(('call', 'Luke Gives the Reason Before the Parable',
   'No question from a hearer introduces this parable. Luke gives the reason in his own words: “And as they heard these things, he added and spake a parable, because he was nigh to Jerusalem, and because they thought that the kingdom of God should immediately appear” (19:11). Luke does not say who “they” are, and this study adds nothing to that.'))
A(('p', '2.3 The reason Luke gives. Luke gives two reasons together: “because he was nigh to Jerusalem, and because they thought that the kingdom of God should immediately appear” (19:11). The word “therefore” opens the next verse: “He said therefore, A certain nobleman went into a far country to receive for himself a kingdom, and to return” (19:12). The parable does not begin with a comparison to the kingdom. Luke’s own words at 19:11 are the only statement he gives of why it was told.'))
A(('p', '2.4 The road before it. Luke has already recorded what Jesus said on the way: “Then he took unto him the twelve, and said unto them, Behold, we go up to Jerusalem, and all things that are written by the prophets concerning the Son of man shall be accomplished” (18:31). Luke adds, “And they understood none of these things: and this saying was hid from them, neither knew they the things which were spoken” (18:34). After the parable Luke writes, “And when he had thus spoken, he went before, ascending up to Jerusalem” (19:28).'))
A(('p', '2.5 The prophetic context. Luke has recorded what Jesus preached and what He sent others to preach. “I must preach the kingdom of God to other cities also: for therefore am I sent” (4:43). “And he sent them to preach the kingdom of God, and to heal the sick” (9:2). The parable of the pounds speaks of a kingdom that a nobleman goes to receive and of citizens who will not have him to reign over them (19:12, 14). It is read within that ministry and within the nearness to Jerusalem that Luke names at 19:11.'))
