# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3
A(('h1', S3))
A(('p', 'The parable is read here in the order Jesus tells it. Nothing is rearranged, nothing is added from another parable, and no meaning is given to any part before the text gives it. The quotations keep the KJV word “pound.”'))
A(('p', '3.1 The opening (19:12). “He said therefore, A certain nobleman went into a far country to receive for himself a kingdom, and to return.” A certain nobleman goes into a far country. He goes for two reasons, to receive a kingdom for himself and to return.'))
A(('p', '3.2 The commission (19:13). “And he called his ten servants, and delivered them ten pounds, and said unto them, Occupy till I come.” He calls ten servants and delivers ten pounds to them. He says one thing to them: “Occupy till I come.” The text does not say how the ten pounds were divided, but the verses that follow speak of “thy pound” (19:16, 20).'))
A(('p', '3.3 The citizens (19:14). “But his citizens hated him, and sent a message after him, saying, We will not have this man to reign over us.” The citizens hated him. They sent a message after him. The message says they will not have this man to reign over them. The text does not say who carried the message or where it was sent.'))
A(('p', '3.4 The return and the reckoning (19:15). “And it came to pass, that when he was returned, having received the kingdom, then he commanded these servants to be called unto him, to whom he had given the money, that he might know how much every man had gained by trading.” He is returned, having received the kingdom. He commands the servants to be called. He gives one reason: that he might know how much every man had gained by trading.'))
A(('p', '3.5 The first and the second (19:16–19). “Then came the first, saying, Lord, thy pound hath gained ten pounds. And he said unto him, Well, thou good servant: because thou hast been faithful in a very little, have thou authority over ten cities. And the second came, saying, Lord, thy pound hath gained five pounds. And he said likewise to him, Be thou also over five cities.” The first reports ten pounds gained and is told, “have thou authority over ten cities.” The second reports five pounds gained and is told, “Be thou also over five cities.” The reason given for the first is, “because thou hast been faithful in a very little.”'))
A(('p', '3.6 The other (19:20–21). “And another came, saying, Lord, behold, here is thy pound, which I have kept laid up in a napkin: For I feared thee, because thou art an austere man: thou takest up that thou layedst not down, and reapest that thou didst not sow.” The text says “another came.” He brings back the pound laid up in a napkin. He gives a reason: he feared, because he says the lord is an austere man who takes up what he did not lay down and reaps what he did not sow. The text reports only three of the ten servants.'))
A(('p', '3.7 The lord’s answer (19:22–23). “And he saith unto him, Out of thine own mouth will I judge thee, thou wicked servant. Thou knewest that I was an austere man, taking up that I laid not down, and reaping that I did not sow: Wherefore then gavest not thou my money into the bank, that at my coming I might have required mine own with usury?” The lord says he will judge the servant out of his own mouth. He calls him “wicked servant.” He repeats the servant’s words about himself and asks why the money was not given into the bank, so that at his coming he might have required his own with usury.'))
A(('p', '3.8 The taking away (19:24–25). “And he said unto them that stood by, Take from him the pound, and give it to him that hath ten pounds. (And they said unto him, Lord, he hath ten pounds.)” The lord speaks to “them that stood by.” He commands that the pound be taken and given to the one who has ten pounds. A parenthesis follows: “Lord, he hath ten pounds.” The text does not say who “them that stood by” are.'))
A(('p', '3.9 The saying (19:26). “For I say unto you, That unto every one which hath shall be given; and from him that hath not, even that he hath shall be taken away from him.” The saying begins with “For.” It states two things, what is given to everyone who has and what is taken from him who has not.'))
A(('p', '3.10 The enemies (19:27). “But those mine enemies, which would not that I should reign over them, bring hither, and slay them before me.” The lord speaks of “those mine enemies, which would not that I should reign over them.” He commands that they be brought hither and slain before him. The text does not say whether these enemies are the citizens of 19:14, and the wording is close but is not joined.'))
A(('p', '3.11 After the story (19:28). “And when he had thus spoken, he went before, ascending up to Jerusalem.” Luke gives no word after the parable except that Jesus went before, ascending up to Jerusalem.'))
