A(('p', '3.12 What the story contains. The text names a nobleman, a far country, a kingdom, ten servants, ten pounds, the citizens, a message, a return, the first, the second and another, trading, ten cities and five cities, a napkin, an austere man, a bank, usury, them that stood by, a saying, and enemies who are brought and slain. The action moves from a going away, to a commission, to a refusal sent after him, to a return, to an accounting, to a taking away, to a command about the enemies.'))
A(('p', '3.13 What the story leaves unsaid. Jesus does not say who the nobleman is. Jesus does not say what the far country is, or what kingdom he receives, or when he returns. Jesus does not say who the citizens are, or who carries their message. Jesus does not say who the servants are, or what the pounds are, or what “occupy” covers beyond trading. Jesus does not say what the ten cities and the five cities are. Jesus does not say who “them that stood by” are or who speaks in the parenthesis. Jesus does not say whether the enemies are the citizens of 19:14. The text reports three servants of the ten and does not report the other seven. The story stops with the command about the enemies.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4
A(('h1', S4))
A(('p', '4.1 Jesus gives no explanation. Luke records no explanation of the parable and no request for one. After the last verse of the story Luke writes, “And when he had thus spoken, he went before, ascending up to Jerusalem” (19:28). This section therefore records no identification, because there is none to record.'))
A(('p', '4.2 What is not an identification. Three things in the parable are easily mistaken for an explanation. Luke’s statement at 19:11 gives the reason the parable was told and is Luke’s own word. The lord’s saying at 19:26, “For I say unto you,” and his command at 19:27 are spoken by the lord inside the story, and they are not Jesus’ statement of what any part stands for. The bystanders’ words at 19:25 are the parenthesis of the story. All four are recorded here as words in the account and not as identifications.'))
A(('p', '4.3 What Jesus does not identify. The parable names many parts of the story and leaves each of them without an explanation. Jesus does not identify the nobleman. Jesus does not identify the far country or the kingdom. Jesus does not identify the ten servants, the first, the second or another. Jesus does not identify the pounds or the trading. Jesus does not identify the citizens or their message. Jesus does not identify the ten cities or the five cities. Jesus does not identify them that stood by. Jesus does not identify the napkin, the bank or the usury. Jesus does not identify the enemies who are brought and slain.'))
