A(('call', 'A Warning Against Adding',
   'Because Jesus gives no identification, none is given here. A reader who says that the nobleman is a certain person, that the far country is a certain place, that the servants are a certain people, that the pounds are a certain gift, that the citizens are a certain nation or that the cities are a certain reward is supplying what the Lord did not.'))
A(('p', '4.4 What the text does say. The text says that a certain nobleman went into a far country to receive for himself a kingdom, and to return; that he delivered ten pounds to ten servants and said “Occupy till I come”; that his citizens hated him and sent a message that they would not have him to reign over them; that he returned, having received the kingdom, and called the servants to know what they had gained; that the first gained ten pounds and was given authority over ten cities, and the second gained five and was given five cities; that another brought back the pound in a napkin, was called a wicked servant, and had the pound taken from him; that the lord said unto every one which hath shall be given, and from him that hath not shall be taken away; and that he commanded his enemies to be brought and slain before him. These statements are made by the story itself and are the only material from which the meaning can be stated.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
A(('h1', S5))
A(('p', 'No explanation is given for this parable, so this section states only the meaning that arises from the comparison itself. It assigns no identity to any part of the story. The parable does not open with a comparison to the kingdom. Luke says that the hearers “thought that the kingdom of God should immediately appear” (19:11), and the story that follows is about a nobleman who goes away to receive a kingdom and to return.'))
A(('p', '5.1 The comparison. A nobleman goes away to receive a kingdom and to return. Before he goes he commits money to ten servants with one charge, “Occupy till I come.” While he is away his citizens refuse him. When he returns, having received the kingdom, he reckons with his servants and deals with his enemies. The meaning is in what the story shows of the going, the charge, the refusal, the return and the reckoning.'))
A(('pk', '5.2 What the comparison communicates.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**A going away and a return stand between the commission and the accounting.** The nobleman goes “to receive for himself a kingdom, and to return” (19:12), and the servants are told, “Occupy till I come” (19:13). The reckoning happens “when he was returned” (19:15). The story does not say how long the absence is.',
    '**A trust is committed and then accounted for.** The servants are given the pounds and then called “that he might know how much every man had gained by trading” (19:15).',
    '**Faithfulness in a little is met with authority.** “Because thou hast been faithful in a very little, have thou authority over ten cities” (19:17). The second is told, “Be thou also over five cities” (19:19).',
    '**A pound laid up unused is taken away.** The servant who kept his pound “laid up in a napkin” is told that out of his own mouth he is judged, and the pound is taken from him (19:20–24).',
    '**Refusing the one who receives the kingdom has consequences.** The citizens say, “We will not have this man to reign over us” (19:14), and the lord speaks of “those mine enemies, which would not that I should reign over them” (19:27).',
]))
A(('p', '5.3 What the hearers were meant to understand. Luke does not name the hearers of the parable. He says only “they” (19:11) and that they thought the kingdom of God should immediately appear. They were meant to understand that a kingdom is received by a nobleman who goes away and comes back, that those left with his money are charged to occupy till he comes, that a refusal can be sent after him, and that the return brings a reckoning for servants and for enemies. Luke does not say that the servants or the citizens of the story are any of these hearers, and Jesus does not identify them.'))
A(('p', '5.4 The force of the comparison. The force is in the reversal between the servants. The one who gained ten and the one who gained five are given cities, and the one who brought back what he was given is stripped of it. The force is also in the lord’s answer to the servant’s own words: he takes the servant’s description of him and asks why the money was not put to the bank. The parable asks the hearer to see that a charge is given before an absence and is answered at the return.'))
A(('p', '5.5 What the comparison supports, in short statements. A trust is committed before the going away. The return brings a reckoning. Faithfulness in a little is given authority. What is laid up unused is taken away. Refusing the one who receives the kingdom has consequences. These statements arise from the comparison, and they do not say that the nobleman, the servants, the citizens, the pounds or the cities represents anyone or anything. The text does not say when the return takes place, and none is stated here.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6
A(('h1', S6))
A(('p', 'The pounds is one of several Gospel parables of servants and an absent lord. It is kept apart from its neighbors here.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Matt 25:14–30', '“a man travelling into a far country, who called his own servants, and delivered unto them his goods”', 'The Talents. A man, three servants, five, two and one talent, “to every man according to his several ability,” a reckoning “after a long time,” and outer darkness. No nobleman, no citizens, no kingdom received, no cities and no enemies. It stands in its own study.'],
    ['Matt 24:45–51', '“Who then is a faithful and wise servant”', 'The faithful and evil servant. One servant set over a household, a lord who delays, and a portion with the hypocrites. No money is delivered and there are no citizens.'],
    ['Luke 12:42–48', '“Who then is that faithful and wise steward”', 'Told in answer to Peter’s question (12:41). A steward over a household, with stripes few and many. It is not an explanation of Luke 19.'],
    ['Luke 13:6–9', '“A certain man had a fig tree planted in his vineyard”', 'The Barren Fig Tree. A tree, a vineyard and a dresser, with one more year. No money, no absence and no return.'],
    ['Matt 8:12; 25:30', '“outer darkness: there shall be weeping and gnashing of teeth”', 'These words are in Matthew’s setting. Luke 19 does not use them.'],
], [0.17, 0.33, 0.50]))
A(('p', '6.1 Differences in setting. The pounds is told in Luke at the end of the road through Jericho, after the house of Zacchæus and “because he was nigh to Jerusalem” (19:1–11). The Talents is told in Matthew in the Olivet teaching (Matthew 24–25). A shared word such as “servants” or “reckoneth” does not make two settings one.'))
A(('p', '6.2 Differences in characters. The pounds has a nobleman, ten servants, citizens who send a message, bystanders and enemies. The Talents has a man travelling and three servants. No character is carried from one parable into another.'))
A(('p', '6.3 Differences in narrative movement. The pounds moves from a nobleman who goes to receive a kingdom, to a message of refusal sent after him, to a return, to an accounting, to a taking away, to the enemies slain before him. The Talents moves from a man travelling, to servants who trade or hide, to a reckoning after a long time, to a servant cast out. None of those movements is read into the other.'))
A(('p', '6.4 Differences in interpretive detail. Four details must not be carried across. First, the citizens, the message and the enemies belong to Luke 19 only and are not read into Matthew 25. Second, the equal pound for each servant in Luke and the unequal talents of Matthew 25:15 are different details and are not made to agree. Third, “ten cities” and “five cities” are not Matthew’s “ruler over many things.” Fourth, the words “Occupy till I come” belong to Luke 19:13 and are not read into the faithful and evil servant of Matthew 24 or Luke 12.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7
A(('h1', S7))
A(('p', '7.1 The original address. Jesus speaks during the earthly ministry in which He preached the kingdom of God (Luke 4:43) and sent others to preach it (9:2). Luke places the parable on the last road to Jerusalem, “because he was nigh to Jerusalem, and because they thought that the kingdom of God should immediately appear” (19:11). The parable belongs to that ministry and to that road.'))
A(('call', 'Standing Statements',
   'Jesus speaks during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. This is a principled lesson, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body.'))
A(('p', '7.2 The Acts overlap boundary. Israel’s prophetic program did not end with the Gospels, and it did not stop when Paul was called. The Kingdom message is still preached to Israel in Acts 1–8, and through Acts 9–28 Israel’s program and the Mystery run side by side. The two never merge, transfer or absorb one another. Israel’s program was nationally suspended at Acts 28. The parable was told before any of that, and it is not read as though the church of the Body were already present.'))
A(('p', '7.3 The Mystery begins with Paul. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. The kingdom a nobleman goes to receive, the servants he charges and the cities he gives are part of what Jesus said on the road to Jerusalem. They are not the Mystery that was hid in God and made known through Paul.'))
A(('p', '7.4 What is not transferred. The nobleman, the kingdom, the pounds, the cities, the servants and the enemies are not the identity of the Body of Christ, and they are not its salvation. The parable is not read as a universal picture of how any person comes to God. Occupying, trading, laying up in a napkin and refusing to be ruled are actions in a story, and they are not a mechanism of salvation for anyone. The authority over ten cities and five cities is not the Body’s inheritance. The Body’s salvation is read in Paul’s letters and not in this parable: “For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: Not of works, lest any man should boast” (Ephesians 2:8–9).'))
A(('p', '7.5 Responsible present learning. A reader today may draw lessons from what the story shows. They are lessons of conduct and not of identity.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**A lesson of stewardship.** The charge is “Occupy till I come” (19:13). A reader learns that what has been committed to him is to be used and not laid up.',
    '**A lesson of faithfulness in little.** The first is told, “thou hast been faithful in a very little” (19:17). A reader learns that small things are taken seriously by the one who gave them.',
    '**A lesson against excuse.** The servant says, “I feared thee” (19:21), and the lord answers out of his own mouth. A reader learns that a poor opinion of the one who gave the trust is not a reason for leaving it unused.',
    '**A lesson of reckoning.** The servants are called “that he might know how much every man had gained” (19:15). A reader learns that a trust is accounted for.',
]))
A(('p', 'These are principled lessons, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body. The Kingdom’s cities and authority are not turned into the Body’s reward, and the lord’s reckoning at his return is not turned into a requirement laid on the Body. Whatever the Body is told about its own calling, its inheritance and the way it is saved is read in Paul’s letters and not in this parable.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8
A(('h1', S8))
A(('p', 'Luke says Jesus spoke a parable because He was nigh to Jerusalem and because they thought the kingdom of God should immediately appear. A certain nobleman went into a far country to receive a kingdom and to return. He gave ten pounds to ten servants and said, “Occupy till I come.” His citizens sent a message after him: “We will not have this man to reign over us.” He returned, having received the kingdom, and called his servants. The first had gained ten pounds and was given authority over ten cities. The second had gained five and was given five cities. Another brought back the pound in a napkin, was called a wicked servant, and had the pound taken from him. The lord said that to every one which hath shall be given, and from him that hath not shall be taken away. He commanded that his enemies be brought and slain before him. Jesus gives no explanation and names none of the parts. The comparison says that a trust is committed before the going away, that the return brings a reckoning, that faithfulness in a little is given authority, that what is laid up unused is taken away, and that refusing the one who receives the kingdom has consequences. Jesus spoke during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap, and the Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Any reading that says who the nobleman, the citizens, the servants, the pounds or the cities are, or that says when the return takes place, goes beyond what the text states.'))
A(('pk', '**Teaching Summary.**'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The sequence:** a nobleman goes to receive a kingdom and to return; ten pounds are given to ten servants; the citizens send a message of refusal; he returns; the servants are called; ten cities and five cities are given; the pound in a napkin is taken away; the enemies are commanded to be slain before him.',
    '**The meaning:** a trust is committed and accounted for; faithfulness in a little is given authority; what is laid up unused is taken away; refusing the one who receives the kingdom has consequences.',
    '**The boundaries:** no explanation from Jesus; no identity for the nobleman, the kingdom, the citizens, the servants, the pounds or the cities; nothing carried in from the Talents or the parables beside it.',
    '**The present lesson:** stewardship, faithfulness in little, no excuse and reckoning, drawn as principles and not as a transfer.',
]))

